# macOS
.DS_Store

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# OpenWork session data, including the original card image that was attached
# to the chat. This is private working material, not part of the site.
.opencode/

# The workbook is the source of truth for the site, but it is a private file:
# once you fill it in it will hold real phone numbers, and the RSVP List sheet
# is meant to hold guests' names and replies. Keep it out of a public repo.
#
# Publishing does not need it. docs/ is already the built site, so GitHub Pages
# deploys fine without this file, and docs/data.js is tracked, so a rebuild still
# goes live on the next push.
#
# To keep a rebuildable copy under version control too, delete the line below --
# but read the note above first, and check the RSVP List sheet holds no real
# guests before you do.
wedding-details.xlsx
~$wedding-details.xlsx
*.xlsx.lock

# Scratch workbooks kept alongside the main one, and the lock file Excel leaves
# while a workbook is open. family_list.xlsx holds relatives' names, so it stays
# out of the repo for the same reason wedding-details.xlsx does.
family_list.xlsx
~$*.xlsx

# The copy handed to the bride, made by make_bride_workbook.py. It is built
# from wedding-details.xlsx and inherits everything in it, including the
# relatives' names and whatever is in the RSVP List sheet, so it is ignored for
# the same reason the main workbook is. It is a file to send to a person, not a
# file to publish: generate it fresh when it is needed rather than committing
# one.
bride-details.xlsx

# The Hindi demo from make_hindi_demo.py. Local only, by design -- it exists so
# the Devanagari typography can be looked at before deciding whether to do a
# real Hindi card. The generated pages and their data file are all ignored so a
# demo can never end up deployed by accident.
docs/hindi-demo-*.html
docs/data-hindi.js

# The bride's version of the card is NOT ignored. It is a real, finished card
# that her family has to be able to open at a link, and it is built from
# bride-details.xlsx by build_site.py. Once it is tracked here, rebuilding
# regenerates the same three files and the diff shows what changed on her card.
#
# Two things follow from that, and both are the point of generating it from the
# workbook rather than hand-editing the pages:
#
#   - data-bride.js is public from the moment it is committed, so it may only
#     ever hold the bride's own text. Her workbook blanks the groom's relatives
#     and the ritual explanations, so the file starts with none of it in it.
#   - bride-details.xlsx itself stays ignored (see above). The spreadsheet holds
#     the RSVP list and real phone numbers; the built data file does not.
